<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ōuchi Yoshitaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1507–1551</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ōuchi Yoshitaka was head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ōuchi clan</a>, lords of Suō and Nagato in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, whose capital Yamaguchi was a centre of courtly culture and of trade with Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> in 1551 and allowed him to preach. Later that year his retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sue-harukata">Sue Harukata</a> rebelled, and Yoshitaka was forced to kill himself, bringing about the clan's fall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshitaka.jpg" alt="Ōuchi Yoshitaka" width="320" height="652" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Inscription by Isetsu Keiju) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%8Cuchi_Yoshitaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
